## 714 Recalls and Counting

The food industry has seen 714 recalls this year alone, with 213 occurring in June. These figures are alarming, especially when you consider that 181 of these were Class I recalls, which denote a reasonable probability that the use of the product will cause serious health consequences or death.

## Top Offenders and Their Missteps

Among the brands with the most recalls, Moonlight Packing Corporation leads with 11 recalls this year, all of which are Class I. This is closely followed by Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, which has had 10 Class I recalls. Both brands have consistently demonstrated lapses in their quality control processes, underscoring the need for stricter oversight.

## A Closer Look at Recent Recalls

Recent recall examples underline the range of issues plaguing the industry. For instance:

- MID DOLCIARIA SRL's gluten-free crunchy cannoli was recalled due to the use of unapproved colors.
- International Food Products Corporation faced multiple recalls for products potentially contaminated with Salmonella.
- Dkiru LLC had undeclared melatonin in their magnesium gummies, which poses risks for consumers who may be unaware of this additive.
- Al Yaman Halawa Extra Pistachio by GREENWORLD FOOD EXPRESS (USA) LLC was recalled for Salmonella contamination.
- Jonco Industries recalled popcorn seasoning made with contaminated milk powder.

These examples highlight recurring issues such as undeclared ingredients, contamination with harmful pathogens, and mislabeling.

## A Call to Action

The trend of increasing recalls and persistent offenders should concern both consumers and regulatory bodies. While the FDA and other agencies work to mitigate these issues, the onus is on manufacturers to adopt rigorous quality control measures and prioritize consumer safety. Until then, consumers are encouraged to stay informed and cautious, particularly when purchasing products from brands with frequent recalls.
